DAY 1 – Programming Foundations + OOPS

50 Interview Questions & Answers

⸻

Basics (Q1–Q10)

Q1. What is programming?
Programming is giving instructions to a computer to perform tasks and solve problems.

Q2. What is a program?
A program is a set of instructions written in a programming language.

Q3. What is software?
Software is a collection of programs used to perform specific tasks.

Q4. What is an algorithm?
An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure to solve a problem.

Q5. What is a programming language?
A language used to communicate instructions to a computer.

Q6. What is source code?
Human-readable code written by a programmer.

Q7. What is compilation?
Converting source code into machine-readable form.

Q8. What is debugging?
Finding and fixing errors in a program.

Q9. What is syntax?
Rules of writing code in a programming language.

Q10. What is logic in programming?
The way conditions and steps are arranged to solve a problem.

⸻

OOPS Fundamentals (Q11–Q25)

Q11. What is OOPS?
OOPS is a programming approach based on objects representing real-world entities.

Q12. Why use OOPS?
For code reusability, scalability, and easier maintenance.

Q13. What are the four pillars of OOPS?
Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, Abstraction.

Q14. What is a class?
A class is a blueprint for creating objects.

Q15. What is an object?
An object is an instance of a class.

Q16. Example of class and object?
Class: Car
Object: BMW, Audi

Q17. What is encapsulation?
Binding data and methods together and controlling access.

Q18. Why is encapsulation important?
It protects data and improves code security.

Q19. What is data hiding?
Restricting direct access to variables using access modifiers.

Q20. How is encapsulation achieved?
Using private variables and public methods.

Q21. What is inheritance?
One class acquiring properties of another class.

Q22. Why inheritance is used?
To reuse code and reduce duplication.

Q23. What is a parent class?
The class whose properties are inherited.

Q24. What is a child class?
The class that inherits properties.

Q25. Can a child class have its own methods?
Yes, it can have additional methods.

⸻

Intermediate OOPS (Q26–Q40)

Q26. What is polymorphism?
Ability of a method to take multiple forms.

Q27. Types of polymorphism?
Compile-time and runtime.

Q28. Example of polymorphism?
Method overloading and overriding.

Q29. What is method overloading?
Same method name with different parameters.

Q30. What is method overriding?
Child class redefining parent class method.

Q31. What is abstraction?
Hiding implementation details and showing only essentials.

Q32. How is abstraction achieved?
Using abstract classes or interfaces.

Q33. Difference between abstraction and encapsulation?
Abstraction hides implementation.
Encapsulation hides data.

Q34. What is an abstract class?
A class that cannot be instantiated and may have abstract methods.

Q35. What is an interface?
A contract that defines methods without implementation.

Q36. Can we create an object of abstract class?
No.

Q37. Why interfaces are used?
To achieve full abstraction.

Q38. Can a class implement multiple interfaces?
Yes.

Q39. Is OOPS language dependent?
No, it is a programming concept.

Q40. Is Java fully object-oriented?
No, because of primitive data types.

⸻

Real-World & Interview Angle (Q41–Q50)

Q41. Give real-life example of encapsulation.
ATM machine hides internal logic and exposes operations.

Q42. Real-life example of inheritance.
Child inherits traits from parents.

Q43. Real-life example of polymorphism.
Same person behaving differently at work and home.

Q44. Where is OOPS used in real projects?
Backend systems, web apps, mobile apps.

Q45. How does OOPS help in large applications?
By organizing code and improving maintainability.

Q46. What happens if OOPS is not used?
Code becomes difficult to manage and reuse.

Q47. Which OOPS concept improves security?
Encapsulation.

Q48. Which OOPS concept improves flexibility?
Polymorphism.

Q49. Which OOPS concept improves scalability?
Inheritance and abstraction.

Q50. Why interviewers focus on OOPS for freshers?
It shows problem-solving ability and coding fundamentals.

